  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nPrecip...Small adjustments/increases in QPF over the next 24 \nhours, otherwise forecast remains similar to the previous update.\n\nMoisture plume associated with sfc low/shortwave in the central\nPlains has been spreading northeast today and resulted in onset of\nwidespread rain across central and northeast WI. Expect steady \nrain to continue this afternoon and evening, followed by rain \nbecoming scattered overnight as two energy areas phase across the \nGreat Lakes and move east. Lingering low-level moisture and \ntrailing surface trough will keep widely scattered rain and \ndrizzle in the forecast throughout Friday morning, and into the \nafternoon for the east. Event total rain amounts will range from \napproximately 0.75 to 1.10 inches, with probabilities of exceeding\n1 inch of rain now ranging from 60 to 90%. Most of this will \noccur before 06Z/Fri, with around 0.20 inch or less after that \ntime. \n\nWind...As northern and southern stream lows approach from the\nnorthwest and southwest, then phase just east of WI, expect\ntightening pressure gradient to result in strong north to\nnorthwest winds on Friday. Gusts of 30 to 35 mph are forecast area\nwide. Potential for wind gusts reaching 45 mph are greatest near \nthe lake shore, where this is a small potential for wind-related \nheadlines. However, right now the probability of exceeding 45 mph \ngusts stands around 30%. See MARINE section for additional wind \ninformation. \n\nTemperatures...Cold front associated with northern stream low will\nusher in a cooler batch of air for a couple days. Clouds and wind\nwill keep temps in the mid 30s and 40s tonight, but clearing skies\nand lights winds on Friday night will allow readings to fall below\nfreezing in most locations. Probabilities range from 40 to 80% for\na hard freeze, lows of 28 degrees or cooler, away from the lake \nshore counties. Therefore, a Freeze Watch was issued for all \ncounties where the growing season continues, aside from Door \nCounty. Temperatures quickly start to rebound on Sunday with highs\nback in the 60s for several days. \n\nNext week...High pressure will result in low impact weather into\nat least the middle of next week, with temperatures AOA normal,\nlittle to no precip chances until at least Thursday, and light\nwinds.",
